Classifying viruses is less about malicious intent and more about their interaction with software. High-profile threats include:** worm/suspicious email attachments**: overwrite contiguous memory locations causing cascading crashes;

Pseudovirus**: use shellcode to lie about what program it is, forcing override hooks for all running processes;

Stalker / rootkit**: remain hidden in the kernel, sending out disguised processes to other machines via Domain Runtime Polling (DRPC). These often rely on valid-looking C2 communication channels to evade detection.

Detectability depends on understanding "behavior": suspicious network traffic spikes—malicious outbound packets often seen via standard DNS enumeration—and unmanaged or orphaned registry keys that consume disk space. The key insight for defense is recognizing when malware is treating your hardware like a resource to be exploited.

